\section{Variable Importance Measures in Random Forests}
\label{sec:vim}

In this section we introduce the motivation for Variable Importance Measures, particularly in Random Forest. High-dimensional data routinely involve hundreds to tens of thousands of features, of which only a small subset are scientifically relevant. Random forests \citep{Breiman2001} is one of the most common
tools for this due to its nonparametric nature, its ability to handle correlated predictors, and produce built-in measures of feature relevance. These measures, typically defined as \textit{variable importance measures} (VIMs), quantify each feature's contribution to predictive accuracy and is the basis of most random forest-based feature selection methods \citep{Genuer2010, Hapfelmeier2014}.

A random forest is an ensemble of $B$ decision trees, each grown on a bootstrap resample of the training data and further randomized by restricting each split to a randomly drawn subset of $m \leq p$ candidate features \citep{Breiman2001}. The double randomization, bootstrap sampling and random feature subspacing, is what allows it to decorrelate the individual trees, reducing variance without substantially increasing bias \citep{Hastie2009}. Predictions are then aggregated by majority vote (classification) or averaging (regression). The original implementations are in \proglang{R} packages \pkg{randomForest} \citep{Liaw2002} and \pkg{ranger} \citep{Wright2017}, the latter providing singificant speed improvements for high-dimensional data through parallelized tree growing.

Two families of VIM have been proposed for random forests, both originating with \citet{Breiman2001}: the \textit{Gini importance} (mean decrease in impurity; MDI) and the \textit{permutation importance} (mean decrease in accuracy; MDA). 

\subsection*{Gini Importance}

The Gini importance of feature $X_j$ aggregates the reduction in node impurity attributable to splits on $X_j$ across all trees:
\begin{equation}
  \widehat{\text{VIM}}_j^{\,\text{G}}
  \;=\;
  \frac{1}{B}
  \sum_{b=1}^{B}
  \sum_{\substack{t \,\in\, \mathcal{T}_b \\ v(t) = j}}
  \Delta i(t),
  \label{eq:gini}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{T}_b$ denotes the internal nodes of tree $b$, $v(t)$ is the splitting variable at node $t$, and $\Delta i(t)$ is the impurity reduction at that node (Gini index for classification, variance for regression). The Gini importance is computed as a by-product of tree growing and requires no additional computation,
making it the default importance measure in many implementations.

However, Equation~\eqref{eq:gini} is subject to selection bias. Because the tree-growing algorithm evaluates all possible split points for each candidate feature,
continuous variables and those with many unique values offer more opportunities to achieve a large $\Delta i(t)$ by chance, independently of their true association with the outcome \citep{Strobl2007}. This implies that Gini importance will overestimate the relevance of high-cardinality features and underestimates that of binary or low-cardinality features, even when no true association exists.
\citet{Strobl2007} demonstrate this bias through simulation and derive its source analytically, showing that it persists regardless of sample size. Because high-dimensional network data routinely contain continuous measurements on thousands of features, this bias makes Gini importance unsuitable as a basis for variable selection in such settings.

\subsection{Permutation Variable Importance}
\label{subsec:perm_vim}

The permutation importance of feature $X_j$, introduced by \citet{Breiman2001}, measures how much the out-of-bag (OOB) prediction error increases when $X_j$'s association with the outcome is broken by random permutation:
\begin{equation}
  \widehat{\text{VIM}}_j^{\,\pi}
  \;=\;
  \frac{1}{B}
  \sum_{b=1}^{B}
  \Bigl(
    \widehat{\text{err}}^{(b)}_{\pi j}
    -
    \widehat{\text{err}}^{(b)}
  \Bigr),
  \label{eq:perm}
\end{equation}
where $\widehat{\text{err}}^{(b)}$ is the OOB prediction error of tree $b$ evaluated on the original data, and $\widehat{\text{err}}^{(b)}_{\pi j}$ is the OOB error of the same tree after $X_j$ has been randomly permuted in the OOB sample. A large positive value of $\widehat{\text{VIM}}_j^{\,\pi}$ indicates that permuting $X_j$ degrades predictions, implying that the tree relies on $X_j$ to achieve low error.

An advantage of Equation~\eqref{eq:perm} is that the comparison is \textit{paired}: both error terms are computed on the identical set of OOB observations for tree $b$, differing only in whether $X_j$ has been permuted. This pairing eliminates tree-level variation in OOB sample composition. This provides an importance estimate with lower variance than a two-sample comparison would provide \citep{Breiman2001}. Under the assumption that features are mutually independent, permutation importance provides an unbiased measure of each feature's marginal predictive contribution \citep{Strobl2008}. These properties have made permutation importance the standard for random forest-based variable selection \citep{Nicodemus2010, Hapfelmeier2014}, and it is the default importance criterion in the \proglang{R} packages \pkg{ranger} \citep{Wright2017} and \pkg{party} \citep{Hothorn2006}.

\subsubsection{Correlation Bias}
\label{subsubsec:corr_bias}

The independence assumption underlying Equation~\eqref{eq:perm} is routinely violated in high-dimensional data. When $X_j$ is correlated with one or more other features, a marginal permutation of $X_j$ does not fully separate its association with the outcome: correlated features $X_k$ retain the predictive information about $\mathbf{y}$ that the permutation was intended to break up \citep{Strobl2008}. The forest compensates by routing predictions through $X_k$, so the measured increase in error after permuting $X_j$ is smaller than it would be under true independence. This presents two bias problems. First, importance is \textit{diluted} among correlated features: each member of a correlated group appears less important than its true contribution warrants, because its signal is partially recoverable from its neighbors. Second, features that share no direct relationship with the outcome but are correlated with a true signal receive \textit{inflated} importance, as the forest absorbs part of the signal from a correlated predictor through them \citep{Strobl2008,
Nicodemus2010}.

\citet{Strobl2008} proposed \textit{conditional permutation importance}, implemented in the \pkg{party} package \citep{Hothorn2006} as the Conditional Inference Forest (CIF).
Rather than permuting $X_j$ marginally across all observations, conditional permutation draws permuted values of $X_j$ within cells defined by the remaining features. This would give the permuted version approximately independent of $X_j$'s neighborhood in the data.
Formally, let $\mathcal{Z}_j$ denote a grid of conditioning variables for $X_j$; the conditional importance is
\begin{equation}
  \widehat{\text{VIM}}_j^{\,\text{cond}}
  \;=\;
  \frac{1}{B}
  \sum_{b=1}^{B}
  \Bigl(
    \widehat{\text{err}}^{(b)}_{\pi j \mid \mathcal{Z}_j}
    -
    \widehat{\text{err}}^{(b)}
  \Bigr),
  \label{eq:cif}
\end{equation}
where $\widehat{\text{err}}^{(b)}_{\pi j \mid \mathcal{Z}_j}$ denotes the OOB error after permuting $X_j$ within strata defined by $\mathcal{Z}_j$. By conditioning on the neighborhood of $X_j$,
the CIF substantially reduces the within-group bias that arises among mutually correlated features \citep{Strobl2008}.

However, Equation~\eqref{eq:cif} does not account for the higher-order structure that organizes correlated features into distinct groups. In genomic applications, features naturally partition into co-expressed modules, that is, clusters of features that are strongly correlated \textit{within} each module but largely independent across modules. In this setting a second, \textit{between-group} bias emerges that CIF does not resolve. Although individual module members each carry a diluted share of the module's collective signal, the module as a whole
occupies many positions in the importance ranking simultaneously. Independent features, even those with true associations with the outcome, have no co-expressed partners to amplify their presence in
the ranking and therefore appear to carry little importance relative to the correlated group. As a result, true signals in the independent pool are systematically crowded out by the collective weight of module members, each of whom inherits a slice of the module's aggregate predictive contribution. We illustrate this empirically in Section~\ref{sec:sim}, where CIF and Random Forest consistently fails to recover independent true signals in the presence of large co-expression modules, despite those signals having comparable marginal associations with the outcome.

\subsection{Weighted Gene Co-Expression Network Analysis}
\label{subsec:wgcna}

The limitations of permutation-based importance measures under correlated
feature structures, especially with network data, motivated the development of methods that model the dependency structure among features before importance is estimated. Weighted Gene Co-Expression Network Analysis \citep[WGCNA;][]{Zhang2005, Langfelder2008} provides such a framework by constructing a weighted gene co-expression network in which edge weights reflect the pairwise correlation between features, raised to a soft thresholding power $\beta$ chosen to approximate scale-free network topology. Features are then clustered into modules using average linkage hierarchical clustering applied to the topological overlap matrix (TOM), a measure of shared neighborhood structure that is more robust to spurious pairwise correlations than raw correlation alone \citep{Langfelder2008}. Features that do not cluster into any coherent module are assigned to a pool, treated as independent of the identified co-expression structure. The resulting module assignments provide a feature modules that is defined by the underlying biological organization of the data. WGCNA has become a standard preprocessing step in high-dimensional genomic analyses \citep{Horvath2011}.

\subsection{Fuzzy Forest}
\label{subsec:fuzzyforest}

\citet{Conn2019} proposed Fuzzy Forest as a random forest-based feature selection method designed for network-structured data.  Fuzzy Forest uses WGCNA module assignments to partition the feature space into groups correlated within and independent between prior to selection. Furthermore, recursive feature elimination (RFE) has been shown as a mitigation for permutation VIM bias as well. RFE iteratively refits the forest on a shrinking feature set to reduce the influence of correlated noise features on importance estimates 
\citep{DiazUriarte2006, Genuer2010}.Then it applies a two-stage procedure: a within-module RFE step reduces each module to a small set of representative features, and a final random forest is fitted on the union of survivors across all modules to produce a final importance ranking. Fuzzy Forest, thus, avoids the most direct form  of between-group misallocation and improves recovery of module-member signals relative, particulary among independent signals, to the standard random forests in network settings \citep{Conn2019}.

Despite this, Fuzzy Forest inherits several limitations from its reliance on permutation-based variable importance. Below we define them.

\subsubsection{Lack of directional importance.}\label{subsubsec:ff_lim_direction}
Permutation VIM quantifies the magnitude of a feature's predictive contribution but provides no information about the direction or sign of that contribution. A feature that consistently negatively affects the outcome receives the same positive importance score as one that consistently promotes it. 

\subsubsection{Unresolved correlation bias.}\label{subsubsec:ff_lim_correlation}
Network-structured data can amplify the correlation bias inherent in permutation VIM through two possible ways. First, co-expression modules identified by WGCNA tend to be large and exhibit high within-module correlation, as the topological overlap criterion clusters features that share a dense neighborhood in the graph \citep{Langfelder2008}. As module size grows and within-module correlation strengthens, the fragmentation of a true signal's importance across correlated partners gets worse: in a module of $q$ tightly correlated features, the causal member's permutation importance is diluted across all $q$ neighbors, each of which absorbs a portion of the signal when the true feature is permuted. Second, most networks exhibit scale-free topology \citep{Zhang2005}, in which a small number of highly connected hub features are correlated with a disproportionately large fraction of module members. Permuting a hub feature leaves its signal recoverable through a large set of correlated partners, producing an underestimation of hub importance that does not occur under weaker or smaller correlation structures.

Fuzzy Forest's within-module RFE step reduces each module to a set of representative survivors before the final global ranking, which partially mitigates within-module dilution. However, the second stage, a global random forest fitted on the union of all module survivors, reintroduces the bias at a coarser level. Because survivors are selected proportionally from each module, a large, densely connected module contributes more candidates to the final ranking than a smaller module with comparable predictive relevance. The final permutation importance scores of those candidates still reflect the scale distortion induced by their within-module correlation, placing them in an
uneven competition with survivors from smaller modules and with independent-pool features. Under weak or moderate correlation structures this bias is modest. As network organization becomes more pronounced the bias in the final global ranking worsens.

\subsubsection{Instability of single-run selection.}\label{subsubsec:ff_lim_stability}
Fuzzy Forest performs a single RFE pass within each module without assessing the reproducibility of the resulting selection across different subsamples of the data. Permutation importance estimates and Random Forest itself are known to exhibit variability across bootstrap resamples, particularly in small-to-moderate samples due to its stochastic nature \citep{Hapfelmeier2014}. A feature selected in one run may fall below the threshold in another, and there is no mechanism within the Fuzzy Forest framework to define stable signals from noise features that happen to score highly on a given sample.

Taken together, these limitations provide three requirements for a new method: \textit{(i)} an importance measure that provides directional estimates and is robust to correlated predictors; \textit{(ii)} an explicit correction for both the within-group dilution bias and the between-group suppression of independent signals; and \textit{(iii)} a stability selection that filters out importance estimates driven by sampling variability. We address all three in ShapleyForest, which replaces permutation importance with exact Shapley values \citep{Lundberg2017}, separates correlated and independent pools throughout the selection procedure via WGCNA module assignments, and embeds the selection within a shadow-stability framework \citep{Meinshausen2010} that requires a feature to pass a null comparison in a predefined fraction of bootstrap iterations before it enters the final stable set.

\subsection{SHAP Values and BorutaSHAP}\label{subsec:shap}

The limitations of permutation importance identified in Section~\ref{subsec:fuzzyforest} motivate an importance measure that is directional, robust to correlated predictors, and grounded in a principled attribution scheme. Shapley values, originating in cooperative game theory \citep{Shapley1953}, provide such a foundation. Consider a set of players $N = \{1, \dots, p\}$ and a value function $v(S)$ that returns the payoff obtained by a coalition $S \subseteq N$. The Shapley value of player $j$ is its average marginal contribution taken over all coalitions that exclude it,
\begin{equation}
  \phi_j
  \;=\;
  \sum_{S \subseteq N \setminus \{j\}}
  \frac{|S|!\,(p - |S| - 1)!}{p!}
  \bigl[\,v(S \cup \{j\}) - v(S)\,\bigr],
  \label{eq:shapley}
\end{equation}
and is the unique attribution satisfying the axioms of efficiency, symmetry, dummy, and additivity \citep{Shapley1953}.

\citet{Lundberg2017} adapt Equation~\eqref{eq:shapley} to model explanation by casting the features of a prediction model as players and the model's expected output as the value function. For an observation $\mathbf{x}_i$, the value of a coalition $S$ is the conditional expectation $v_i(S) = \mathbb{E}[\,f(\mathbf{x}) \mid \mathbf{x}_S = \mathbf{x}_{i,S}\,]$, so that the local SHAP attribution $\phi_{ij}$ measures how much feature $j$ moves the prediction for observation $i$ away from the baseline. These attributions are additive, $f(\mathbf{x}_i) = \phi_0 + \sum_{j=1}^{p} \phi_{ij}$, and, crucially, \emph{signed}: a feature that pushes the prediction downward receives a negative attribution and one that pushes it upward a positive attribution. A global importance measure follows by averaging magnitudes across the sample, $\Phi_j = \tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} |\phi_{ij}|$, while the sign of $\phi_{ij}$ retains the directional information that permutation importance discards. This directly addresses the first requirement of Section~\ref{subsec:fuzzyforest}.

Computing Equation~\eqref{eq:shapley} exactly is infeasible for general models, as it requires evaluating $2^{p}$ coalitions. For tree ensembles, however, \citet{Lundberg2020} introduce \emph{TreeSHAP}, an algorithm that computes exact Shapley values in time polynomial in the number of trees, leaves, and features, making SHAP attribution practical for random forests on high-dimensional data. \citet{Lundberg2020} further show that TreeSHAP importance is \emph{consistent}, in the sense that a feature's attributed importance never decreases when the model is changed to rely on it more, a guarantee that neither Gini nor permutation importance satisfies.

The symmetry axiom underlying Equation~\eqref{eq:shapley} also shapes how SHAP behaves under correlation. Because two features that make identical marginal contributions receive identical attributions, redundant correlated predictors \emph{share} their common credit rather than one arbitrarily dominating, as can happen when a single tree split is chosen among near-equivalent candidates. This yields fairer and more stable importance estimates within correlated groups than permutation importance provides. Sharing credit is not the same as recovering it, however: within a large co-expression module the module's collective signal is still divided among its members, so the within-group dilution described in Section~\ref{subsubsec:corr_bias} is reduced but not eliminated, and the between-group competition against independent signals remains. SHAP therefore improves the fairness and directionality of importance but, on its own, does not resolve the correlation bias that motivates a module-aware procedure.

A complementary line of work uses SHAP within an explicit selection procedure. \citet{Kursa2010} propose \pkg{Boruta}, an \emph{all-relevant} feature selection algorithm built on \emph{shadow features}: the design matrix is augmented with permuted copies of each feature, whose association with the outcome is broken by construction, and a random forest is fitted on the combined matrix. A real feature is judged relevant in a given run only if its importance exceeds the maximum importance attained by any shadow feature, and this comparison is repeated across many runs and assessed with a binomial test. The shadow features thus supply a data-driven null against which genuine signal must stand out. \citet{Keany2020} combine this scheme with SHAP in \pkg{BorutaShap}, replacing Boruta's default importance with TreeSHAP importance and thereby inheriting its consistency, directionality, and computational efficiency.

BorutaSHAP supplies the two ingredients that ShapleyForest builds upon: a consistent, directional SHAP importance measure and a shadow-feature null that turns importance into a principled selection rule. It nonetheless retains the shortcomings identified for Fuzzy Forest. It operates on all features jointly and does not exploit the module structure recovered by WGCNA, so the between-group suppression of independent signals persists; it makes no distinction between correlated and independent pools; and its relevance decisions, though repeated internally, are evaluated on a single data set rather than across resamples, offering no guarantee that a selected feature is stable under sampling variability. ShapleyForest retains the shadow-SHAP comparison at the core of BorutaSHAP but embeds it within a module-aware, stability-selection framework that addresses all three requirements set out in Section~\ref{subsec:fuzzyforest}.

\section{ShapleyForest}\label{sec:sf}

\subsection{The Algorithm}\label{subsec:sf_alg}

Let $\mathbf{X} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times p}$ denote the feature matrix and $\mathbf{y}$ the response, numeric for regression or a factor for classification. We assume a module assignment that partitions the $p$ features into $G$ co-expression modules $\mathcal{M}_1, \dots, \mathcal{M}_G$ together with an independent pool $\mathcal{I}$ of features that cluster into no module; in practice this partition is supplied by WGCNA (Section~\ref{subsec:wgcna}), but any grouping may be used. Throughout, $\Phi_j = \tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\phi_{ij}|$ denotes the global TreeSHAP importance of feature $j$ and $\bar{\phi}_j = \tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\phi_{ij}$ its signed mean, both computed with the exact TreeSHAP algorithm of \citet{Lundberg2020}. ShapleyForest proceeds in three phases: a module-stratified screening phase that reduces each group independently, a shadow-stability selection phase that retains only reproducibly relevant features, and a final attribution phase that produces directional importances on the stable set. Algorithm~\ref{alg:sf} summarizes the procedure.

\paragraph{Phase 1: Module-stratified screening.}
The screening phase applies SHAP-based recursive feature elimination \citep{DiazUriarte2006, Genuer2010} \emph{separately within each group}. For a group $g \in \{\mathcal{M}_1, \dots, \mathcal{M}_G, \mathcal{I}\}$ with feature matrix $\mathbf{X}_{\cdot, g}$, the survivor set is initialized to all members of $g$; at each iteration a random forest is fitted on the current survivors, their importances $\Phi_j$ are computed by TreeSHAP, and the lowest-ranked $\lceil \texttt{drop\_fraction} \cdot m \rceil$ of the $m$ current features are discarded. The loop terminates once at most $\lceil \texttt{keep\_fraction} \cdot |g| \rceil$ features remain, yielding a per-group survivor set $\mathrm{surv}(g)$. The survivor pool is the union $\mathcal{S} = \bigcup_g \mathrm{surv}(g)$. Because elimination is stratified by group, features compete only against others in the same module during screening. A large, densely connected module therefore cannot displace a smaller module or an independent signal from the survivor pool, which directly counters the between-group crowding-out described in Section~\ref{subsubsec:corr_bias}.

\paragraph{Phase 2: Shadow-stability selection.}
The selection phase evaluates the reproducibility of each survivor against a data-driven null built from shadow features, in the spirit of \pkg{Boruta} \citep{Kursa2010} and stability selection \citep{Meinshausen2010}. Given a feature pool $\mathcal{P} \subseteq \mathcal{S}$ and a working response $\mathbf{y}$, the shadow-stability routine repeats the following for $b = 1, \dots, B$ bootstrap replicates ($B = \texttt{n\_boots}$). A subsample $I_b$ of $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor$ rows is drawn without replacement. A shadow matrix $\tilde{\mathbf{X}}$ is formed by permuting each column of $\mathbf{X}_{I_b, \mathcal{P}}$ independently across rows, which destroys any association with $\mathbf{y}$ while preserving each feature's marginal distribution. A random forest is fitted on the augmented design $[\,\mathbf{X}_{I_b, \mathcal{P}} \mid \tilde{\mathbf{X}}\,]$ of dimension $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor \times 2|\mathcal{P}|$, and TreeSHAP importances are computed for all $2|\mathcal{P}|$ columns. The bootstrap-specific threshold is the upper percentile of the shadow importances,
\begin{equation}
  \tau_b \;=\; Q_{\texttt{shadow\_percentile}}\bigl(\{\,\Phi_j : j \text{ is a shadow feature}\,\}\bigr),
  \label{eq:shadow_thr}
\end{equation}
and a real feature is counted as selected in replicate $b$ if it exceeds its own null, $\Phi_j > \tau_b$. Aggregating across replicates gives the stability frequency
\begin{equation}
  \widehat{\Pi}_j \;=\; \frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B} \mathbf{1}\!\left[\,\Phi_j^{(b)} > \tau_b\,\right].
  \label{eq:stability_freq}
\end{equation}
A feature enters the stable set when $\widehat{\Pi}_j$ exceeds a pool-specific cutoff. Correlated pools use the fixed stability-selection threshold $\widehat{\Pi}_j \ge \texttt{pi\_thr}$ \citep{Meinshausen2010}; the independent pool, whose true signals often carry weaker but genuine effects, uses a data-driven elbow on the sorted frequency curve (or an optional fixed threshold $\texttt{pi\_thr\_indep}$), a more lenient rule that avoids discarding real independent signals against the stringent shadow null.

Two pooling strategies determine how survivors are grouped for the routine above. Under \texttt{shadow\_mode = "split"} (the default), the correlated pool is the union of all module survivors and the independent pool is $\mathcal{I} \cap \mathcal{S}$; the two are evaluated separately and the stable set is $\mathcal{S}^\star = \text{stable}_{\text{corr}} \cup \text{stable}_{\text{ind}}$. Under \texttt{shadow\_mode = "within\_module"}, the routine is run once per module and $\mathcal{S}^\star$ is the union of the per-module stable sets. Separating correlated from independent features prevents the shadow null of a large module from setting the bar for independent signals, addressing the between-group bias at the selection stage.

\paragraph{Phase 2a: Optional debiasing (DML).}
Even after stratification, an independent signal with a modest marginal effect can be masked by the aggregate predictive weight of the correlated modules. When \texttt{use\_dml\_residual = TRUE}, ShapleyForest removes this confounding by partialling out the correlated contribution before the independent-pool stability run, following the double/debiased machine learning framework \citep{Robinson1988, Chernozhukov2018}. The response is replaced by cross-fitted residuals: the rows are split into $K = \texttt{dml\_n\_folds}$ folds, and for each fold the response is predicted from the correlated survivors using a random forest trained on the remaining folds, giving out-of-fold predictions $\hat{g}_{-k}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{corr}})$ such that no observation influences its own residual,
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{y}^{\text{res}} \;=\; \mathbf{y} - \hat{g}_{\text{cv}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{corr}}).
  \label{eq:dml}
\end{equation}
Because $\mathbf{y}^{\text{res}}$ has the correlated-module signal removed, the independent pool's effective signal-to-noise ratio is increased and its shadow-stability comparison is no longer confounded by the modules. The scope of this correction is controlled by \texttt{dml\_scope}: the default \code{"indep"} residualizes only the independent-pool response, whereas \code{"all\_modules"} (mDML) generalizes the correction so that each pool's response is residualized on the complementary features, for example residualizing the correlated pool's response on the independent features. The final attribution phase always uses the original, unresidualized $\mathbf{y}$.

\paragraph{Phase 3: Final attribution.}
A single random forest is fitted on the stable set $\mathbf{X}_{\cdot, \mathcal{S}^\star}$ and explained with exact TreeSHAP, producing a signed matrix $\mathbf{\Phi} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times |\mathcal{S}^\star|}$ of per-observation attributions. From $\mathbf{\Phi}$ the method reports the global importance $\Phi_j$ for ranking, the signed mean $\bar{\phi}_j$ for the direction of each feature's effect, per-observation confidence intervals for both quantities, and the TreeSHAP interaction matrix used by \fct{detect\_interaction} and \fct{plot\_potential\_interactions}. Fitting the forest once on the reduced stable set, rather than repeatedly during selection, keeps the exact-SHAP computation tractable while retaining the consistency and directionality guarantees of Section~\ref{subsec:shap}.

